        border: 0;
        border-radius: 0;
        padding: 0.9rem 1rem;
        box-shadow:
            inset 1px 1px 0 0 var(--border-strong),
            inset -1px -1px 0 0 var(--popover),
            inset 2px 2px 0 0 var(--popover),
            inset -2px -2px 0 0 var(--border-strong);
    }

    /* …and the caption on the groove, the way a 1995 group box cut its
       line for the words (Kenny's retro notes, 2026-09-15, field.html#narrow:
       "the text 'Is anyone at risk?' is struck through by the border of
       the box below it"). A fieldset paints its box from the middle of its
       legend down, and the groove above is a set of inset shadows, which —
       unlike a border — no browser interrupts behind a legend: the two top
       hairlines ran through the middle of the caption's letters. Measured
       in firefox and chromium: the dark hairline crossed "Is anyone at
       risk?" 10.8px below the legend's top, on a legend 21.6px tall.

       So a fieldset with a legend draws no top edge of its own — its other
       three edges are the same pixels as background layers, because inset
       shadows whose top meets a box top at a fractional position leave a
       faint antialiased seam across it (measured 168 against 185 on a
       ground of 191 in firefox) — and the legend draws the top edge instead, left and right of its words: the legend
       spans the fieldset's whole width, its words sit where they sat, and
       its two pseudo-elements are the hairlines, each placed at 50% of the
       legend's height — the same line the fieldset's box starts on. A
       ground colour behind the words, the usual answer, would show as a
       flat patch on this theme's dithered ground. */
    [data-theme='retro'] .kp-fieldset:has(> legend) {
        box-shadow: none;
        background:
            linear-gradient(var(--border-strong), var(--border-strong)) left top / 1px 100% no-repeat,
            linear-gradient(var(--popover), var(--popover)) right top / 1px 100% no-repeat,
            linear-gradient(var(--popover), var(--popover)) left bottom / 100% 1px no-repeat,
            linear-gradient(var(--popover), var(--popover)) left 1px top / 1px 100% no-repeat,
            linear-gradient(var(--border-strong), var(--border-strong)) right 1px top / 1px 100% no-repeat,
            linear-gradient(var(--border-strong), var(--border-strong)) left bottom 1px / 100% 1px no-repeat;
    }

    [data-theme='retro'] .kp-fieldset > legend {
        display: flex;
        align-items: baseline;
        column-gap: 3px;
        box-sizing: border-box;
        inline-size: calc(100% + 2rem);
        margin-inline: -1rem;
    }

    [data-theme='retro'] .kp-fieldset > legend::before,
    [data-theme='retro'] .kp-fieldset > legend::after {
        content: '';
        flex: 1 1 0;
        align-self: stretch;
        min-inline-size: 3px;
        background:
            linear-gradient(to bottom, transparent 50%, var(--border-strong) 50% calc(50% + 2px), transparent calc(50% + 2px)) left / 1px 100%
