@layer kp.register {
    /* @kp-soft/themes — css/dark-register.css
   The dark register, 5.0.0 [S48, LIFT_PLAN row 15]: the ordinary dark
   theme, done properly. Scoped to [data-theme='dark'] and inert
   elsewhere; opt-in, imported after themes.css. The approved concept
   demo ("Small Hours", stars II, 2026-09-08) was the reference for every
   value here.

   THE STARFIELD IS GONE [Kenny, 2026-09-11]: "dark mag zijn sterren weer
   kwijtspelen op de achtergrond". What stood here until that day was 112
   individually-generated positions pasted verbatim from the demo's own
   header — a 102-point field on the shared texture layer at 0.35, and
   ten brighter stars on a layer of their own, their SVG shimmer rebuilt
   as masked conic gradients. None of it was animated; it was a still
   photograph, painted once. It is recorded here rather than only in the
   history because the demo that approved it still shows it, and anyone
   comparing the two deserves to know which way the difference runs.

   The divider keeps its own dense local sample. That is a seam between
   sections, not the background he named, and it stays until he says
   otherwise.

   What this file does, in order: the knobs; the two-channel focus ring,
   reversed from the base default; the type; the hooks of S45 — the
   headline that resolves word by word out of a blur, the mark as an
   ignite (the lede's colour-only, scroll-bound; the dossier's a redact
   bar that lifts on the trigger), the rule that sweeps in under a
   heading, the divider as a night seam with its own dense local sample;
   the nav as a quiet strip with its dropdown's own keyboard ring; the
   buttons, fields and panels; and one answer per component root (TH124).

   Colour discipline (DI9): nothing here names a colour. Every stop is
   `var(--token)` or `hsl(from var(--token) h s l / alpha)`. */

    [data-theme='dark'] {
        /* The month heatmap's nav goes on two rows below this width: the
           widest month and the three buttons in this type [fix-98]. */
        --kp-calendar-nav-fit: 25rem;
        /* A key-figure tile's frame in a strip with columns: --border-strong
           reads 2.22:1 on the page and 2.11:1 on the card here (DI1, open), so
           a fifth of the ink goes in it: 3.55:1 and 3.36:1 [fix-101]. */
        --kp-kpi-border: color-mix(in oklab, var(--border-strong) 80%, var(--foreground));
        /* The configuration surface [AR43], the demo's values as defaults. */
        --kp-reveal-threshold: 0.6;
        --kp-word-stagger: 28ms;
        --kp-redact-stagger: 90ms;
        --kp-sweep-range-start: entry 10%;
        --kp-sweep-range-end: entry 85%;
        --kp-ignite-range-start: entry 30%;
        --kp-ignite-range-end: entry 80%;
        /* The reveals this theme performs [S45]: js/effects.js reads these
           and leaves a theme that declares none at rest. No arrival: the
           demo has no boot sequence, the page is simply there. */
        --kp-reveal-headline: resolve;
        --kp-reveal-emphasis: ignite;
        --kp-reveal-rule: sweep;
        /* Dead North's shout-in ease, reused for the word resolve [S49]. */
        --kp-settle: cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1);

        /* No starfield. The approved demo had one — 102 points on the
           shared texture layer with ten shimmer stars above it — and
           Kenny took it back out on 2026-09-11: "dark mag zijn sterren
           weer kwijtspelen op de achtergrond". The ground is plain now.
           The divider keeps its own dense local sample, which is a seam
           between sections rather than the background he named. */

        /* ── The oxide film [scope-16] ─────────────────────────────────
           The one mechanism this theme is really about. Anodising does not
           add pigment: it grows a film whose thickness decides which
           wavelength survives, so the colour shifts with the angle you
           look from. The gradient's start angle follows the pointer, and
           `js/effects.js` writes that angle down — a gradient cannot read
           a pointer.

           `--kp-px` carries its own value so the gradient is valid before
           anything has moved, and so it keeps working when the module is
           detached or absent. Half a turn is the middle of the sweep. */
        --kp-pointer: track;
        --kp-px: 0.5;
        --kp-py: 0.5;
        --kp-iris: conic-gradient(from calc(var(--kp-px) * 1turn), var(--chart-1), var(--chart-2), var(--chart-3), var(--chart-4), var(--chart-1));

        /* ── The halo: the film, spilled onto the ground [scope-102] ────
           Kenny, 2026-09-16, the shadow-and-ink form, dark-shadow "Gloed
           van de oxidefilm": a raised panel is separated from the ground
           by the film's own four wavelengths rather than by a shadow in
           the ground's own colour, which on a 5%-lightness ground had
           almost nowhere to go (measured 0.4% of the band past ΔL* 3;
           research/dark-dialog-shadow/README.md, option 1).

           Four fixed shadows, not the blurred film itself. The research
           measured the blurred version higher (88.3% of the band at dPR
           1 against this one's share), but it cannot be worn by the two
           surfaces this decision is mostly about: `.kp-popover` is
           `overflow: auto` and a modal dialog is `overflow: auto` by the
           UA stylesheet (HTML Standard §15.3.3), and overflow clips a
           pseudo-element — the blurred halo measured 0 of 17616 band
           pixels on the popover. A box-shadow is not a descendant and is
           not clipped, so the same idea is painted as four of them, one
           per wavelength, placed where the conic gradient puts that
           colour: chart-2 below, chart-1 to the left, chart-3 to the
           right, chart-4 above. The cost, stated rather than hidden: a
           fixed shadow does not turn with the pointer as the film's own
           border does. */
        --kp-halo:
            0 10px 18px hsl(from var(--chart-2) h s l / 0.3), -10px 4px 16px hsl(from var(--chart-1) h s l / 0.26),
